{% extends 'site/base.html.twig' %}
{% set active = 'privacy' %}

{% block title %}Privacy · Aloha Directory{% endblock %}
{% block description %}What the Aloha Directory knows, what it does not know, and how long it keeps data.{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
    <article class="prose">
        <h1>Privacy</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            The directory is built to know as little as possible. It publishes lists of servers; it is not part of signing
            up, posting or searching.
        </p>

        <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
        <p>
            The directory is run by Nextcloud GmbH, Germany, so the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) applies.
            The directory processes the personal data of server admins and of people who send a report. Data of people
            who use a server is the responsibility of that server, under its own privacy policy.
        </p>

        <h2>What the directory never learns</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Who signs up where.</strong> The Aloha apps download the signed list and then talk to the server you choose directly.</li>
            <li><strong>What anyone searches for.</strong> Searches go from your server to a search provider, not through the directory.</li>
            <li><strong>What you do on this website.</strong> There are no cookies, no analytics and no third-party content on these pages.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What the directory holds, and for how long</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
                <caption class="visually-hidden">Data held by the directory</caption>
                <thead>
                    <tr><th scope="col">Data</th><th scope="col">Held</th><th scope="col">Kept for</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Server profiles and heartbeats</td><td>Yes; they are public facts about the servers</td><td>Profiles while listed; heartbeats 90 days, then only daily totals</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Admin contact for each server</td><td>Yes</td><td>While the server is enrolled, plus 1 year</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Curator notes and decisions</td><td>Yes; delisting reasons are public</td><td>Permanently, in the audit log</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Reports about servers</td><td>Yes: the server, category and text</td><td>As part of the curators' records</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Email address in a report (optional)</td><td>Only if you give one</td><td>Deleted 90 days after the report is closed</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Who signed up where</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Signup goes straight from the app to the server</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Search queries</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td>Queries go from the server to the search provider</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>IP addresses of list downloads</td><td>Only to block abuse</td><td>At most 24 hours</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2>List downloads</h2>
        <p>
            The list files log only aggregate counts. They set no cookies. IP addresses are kept for at most 24 hours, and
            only to block abuse. The apps also ship with a copy of the list, and the lists are mirrored on GitHub, so you
            do not have to contact the directory at all.
        </p>

        <h2>Reports</h2>
        <p>
            When you <a href="/report">report a server</a>, the curators see what you wrote and, if you give one, your email
            address. The server admin sees the text of the complaint, but never your email address or any other detail
            about who you are.
        </p>

        <h2>Your rights</h2>
        <p>
            Under the GDPR you can ask for access to, correction or deletion of your personal data, object to its use, and
            complain to a data protection supervisory authority. To use these rights, contact Nextcloud GmbH.
        </p>
    </article>
{% endblock %}
